    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures). Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\n`capacityWarning` is `true` on an owner's response when their city already holds **40\u201349** owners (approaching the limit of 50); the hard 409 rejection at 50 is untouched.\n\n## How it fits the existing design\n\nI followed the established pattern for `bulkSignupWarning` \u2014 a derived, non-persisted DTO field computed at response time by a shared helper \u2014 rather than adding entity state:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the Owner schema (which generates `OwnerDto.setCapacityWarning`), mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n2. **`Cities`** \u2014 this was already the per-city matcher. I extended it to own all \"owners per city\" concerns (paralleling how `DailyRegistrations` owns day-counting + its thresholds): the `count(...)` walk plus `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY` (50), `CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD` (40), `isAtCapacity(...)`, and `isApproachingCapacity(...)` (`count >= 40 && count < 50`).\n\n3. **`EnsureCityCapacity`** \u2014 refactored to delegate to `Cities.isAtCapacity(...)`, removing its private counting loop and the duplicated `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY` constant. Behaviour is identical.\n\n4. **`RespondWithOwner` / `RespondWithOwnerCreated` / `RespondWithOwnerUpdated`** \u2014 each now also sets `capacityWarning` via `Cities.isApproachingCapacity(...)`, right beside the existing `bulkSignupWarning` line, so the create, read, and update responses stay consistent.\n\nThe hard limit and the warning now read the same count through one place, with no logic duplicated across classes.",
